"full_text": "Excellencies, dear colleagues and friends,\nGood morning, good afternoon and good evening to all Member States, and thank you for joining us once again.\nIn just three weeks’ time, on the 17th of December, I will join His Excellency President Emmanuel Macron in Lyon to formally open the WHO Academy.\nThis ceremony, which will be livestreamed, is the culmination of a journey that began seven years ago.\nIn 2017, shortly after my election as Director-General, we set about transforming WHO into an organization that was more responsive to the needs of countries, and more effective in meeting those needs.\nOur Transformation – which is ongoing – has included major changes to our strategy, operating model, processes, financing, partnerships, culture and workforce.\nThe WHO Academy belongs to this final pillar of the Transformation – it aims to build the capacities of our own workforce, but also that of Member States.\nWhen we ask our Member States and our own workforce what they need, the consistent answer is training and capacity building.\nWhile WHO has had programmes to support capacity building before, it has never been institutionalised or implemented at scale.\nThat is what the WHO Academy is about: delivering lifelong learning and capacity building at the scale that Member States and the WHO workforce need for health impact globally.\nOur vision is simple yet powerful: a world where every health and care worker, policymaker, and WHO team member is equipped with the skills and competencies they need to achieve health for all.\nThe COVID-19 pandemic reminded us of the incredible value of health workers, but it also exposed critical gaps in the global health workforce.\nCurrently, the world is facing a shortage estimated at 10 million workers.\nDelivering lifelong learning to health workers is a way to bridge that gap, enhance the quality of care, and to incorporate the latest scientific 
... [truncated, total 9399 chars]
