"full_text": "Organizers: Office of Internal Oversight Services – WHO; Office of Inspector General - The Global Fund, Gavi; Office of Audit and Investigative - Gavi, The Vaccine Alliance\nDear co-chairs, Lucy Elliot, Lisa McClennon, and Tracy Staines,\nDear colleagues and friends,\nGood afternoon, it’s an honour to be with you.\nMy thanks to the Global Fund and Gavi for co-chairing this meeting with WHO, and to all of you for your dedication to making our organizations accountable for the results we deliver.\nJust this morning I returned from Chad, where I had the opportunity to travel to the border with Sudan to see firsthand the growing refugee crisis caused by the conflict in Sudan.\nI was heartbroken by the suffering I saw, but also heartened by the work that our colleagues are doing from WHO, Unicef, UNHCR, OCHA and many others to alleviate that suffering.\nAs auditors, your work often goes unseen and underappreciated.\nBut I want you to know that it is absolutely critical to the work that is happening on the ground.\nBecause you are the guardians of integrity, accountability, and transparency.\nAnd together, they are the ingredients of the most precious commodity we have: trust - the trust of our Member States, donors, and most importantly, the trust of the communities we serve.\nWhen that trust is at risk, everything is at risk.\nAnd when it is broken, the consequences can be huge.\nIn 2018, during the Ebola outbreak in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, WHO personnel were accused of sexual exploitation and abuse.\nThe credibility, the funding and even the very future of the organization were put at risk.\nWe had to start by facing the horror of what had been done by our own people. We listened to the voices of survivors, and we listened to the hard questions our Member States asked us.\nWe learned valuable lessons, and made vital changes to improve the work of the organization, and rebuild
... [truncated, total 8574 chars]
